<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The I/O half of the triad that is NOT Revit (HPS-02). Everything the plugin does to the outside
    world lives here — HTTP, the loopback listener, the bundle cache, the secret store, the zip —
    and none of it touches the Revit API.

    Why a third assembly rather than two. The triad splits pure from impure, and the shim was
    carrying both kinds of impure: "talks to Revit" and "talks to the disk and the network". Only the
    first genuinely needs a licensed install to exercise. Folding the second into the shim put
    ⛔HPS-26 (write to .part, verify, rename) behind `agent-review` alone, because CI cannot build the
    shim on a hosted runner. Split out, the promote-by-rename path is built and tested by
    ci-revit-tests like anything else — the rule's second enforcer, `automation-test`, becomes real.

    net8.0 and not net8.0-windows: nothing here is Windows-only except the DPAPI secret store, which
    carries [SupportedOSPlatform("windows")] and a null-store fallback (HPS-16) that the suite
    exercises on the Linux runner. Keeping the TFM portable is what keeps that fallback honest
    instead of theoretical.

    NO NUGET PACKAGES, same as the rest of the tree (../../Directory.Build.props).
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>MantlePlace.Revit.Client</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>MantlePlace.Revit.Client</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MantlePlace.Revit.Core\MantlePlace.Revit.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
